The 5 seconds cover the whole request, including reading the body (`res.json()`), not only the wait for headers. `AbortSignal.timeout` needs Node 18+ or a browser from 2022 onwards; on older runtimes use an `AbortController` with `setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 5000)`.

```js
function throttle(fn, wait) {
  let last = 0;
  let timer;
  return (...args) => {
    const remaining = wait - (Date.now() - last);
    clearTimeout(timer);
    if (remaining <= 0) {
      last = Date.now();
      fn(...args);
    } else {
      // trailing call, so the final scroll position is not missed
      timer = setTimeout(() => {
        last = Date.now();
        fn(...args);
      }, remaining);
    }
  };
}

window.addEventListener("scroll", throttle(onScroll, 200), { passive: true });
```

It runs immediately on the first event, then at most once per 200 ms, plus one last call after scrolling stops. Keep a reference to the throttled function if you need `removeEventListener` later. If lodash is already installed, `_.throttle(onScroll, 200)` does the same.
